      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "High glycemic index (GI) foods are quickly digested and cause a sudden spike in blood sugar levels, triggering an insulin response to bring blood sugar back under control. Low GI foods, on the other hand, are digested more slowly, resulting in a gradual increase in blood sugar and a lesser insulin response. For someone managing type 2 diabetes, a low GI diet can help regulate blood sugar levels and improve insulin sensitivity. Foods with a GI value of 55 or less are generally considered low GI, while those above 70 are considered high GI.",
